 ## 🤖 AI Workflow Decision Matrix
@@ -166,8 +171,47 @@ newsletter-orchestrator/
 - `POST /api/workflows/newsletter/{id}/run-ai` - Run AI workflow ✨
 
 ### Automation
-- `POST /api/automation/enable` - Enable automation
-- `POST /api/automation/disable` - Disable automation
+- `GET /api/automation/status` - Automation status
+- `POST /api/automation/start` - Start automation
+- `POST /api/automation/stop` - Stop automation
+- `POST /api/automation/tick` - Run one automation tick
+
+### Schedules
+- `POST /api/schedules/{id}/run-now` - Run a schedule immediately
+- `POST /api/schedules/scan-due` - Run one scheduler scan
+- `GET /api/schedules/{id}/runs` - Run history (including `failed` runs and why)
+
+## 📬 Email delivery
+
+Every subscriber receives **their own message** (their address is the only one in `To:`), personalised from their draft. Drafts are marked `sent`/`failed`, so re-running a newsletter never re-emails people who already got it, and a partly failed run can be retried safely.
+
+**Local testing (default).** `EMAIL_MODE=mock` sends to Mailpit (`docker compose` in `infrastructure/` starts it). Open http://127.0.0.1:8025 to see the mail.
+
+**Real delivery.**
+
+```bash
+export EMAIL_MODE=smtp
+export EMAIL_FROM=news@your-verified-domain.org
+export SMTP_HOST=smtp.your-provider.example
+export SMTP_USERNAME=...        # keep secrets out of git
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=...
+# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls (default, port 587) | ssl (port 465)
+```
+
+Certificates are always verified and a server that does not offer STARTTLS is refused rather than silently downgraded. Use your provider's SMTP credentials, and send a test to yourself before using real subscribers.
+
+## ⏱️ Running the automation
+
+Automatic delivery needs **two processes** besides the database/Mailpit; the API alone does not send scheduled newsletters:
+
+```bash
+python -m uvicorn app.main:app --port 8000      # API
+python -m app.scheduler_worker                   # scheduler (scans every 60 s)
+```
+
+A schedule fires from its delivery time until `SCHEDULE_CATCHUP_MINUTES` later, so a restart or short outage does not lose it, and each delivery runs at most once. If a run fails (for example the mail server is down) it is recorded with status `failed` and the reason in `GET /api/schedules/{id}/runs`; it is **not** retried automatically. Other run statuses include `sent`, `partial_failure`, `pending_review` and `skipped_low_relevance`.
+
+Settings are read from the process environment; `.env.example` is a template and is not loaded automatically.
 
